 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<p>“The centrality of railroads in the expansion and development of the United States was used morally and politically to justify the suspension of the laws of laissez-faire and the direct involvement of both federal and state governments in their construction. Similar arguments had been used to justify the National Road. States were involved in promoting and facilitating rail construction right from the start. The federal government began its participation in 1850 by helping the Illinois Central. The real subsidies began, however . . . in 1862, as a result of the Civil War, during which it began to seem lawful and natural for Washington to be involved in everything.”</p><p>\n</p><p align=\"right\"><i>A History of the American People</i>, Paul Johnson, 1997</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
